<section class="reference-section" id="prahlada-narasimha"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Prahlada and Narasimha · Devotion Against Tyranny</h2><a href="/prahlada-narasimha.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition, first volume.</strong> Original English retelling based only on Skandha 7, chapters 3–10 of the owner-supplied edition. The book scan is not distributed by this website.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Open the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>7.3.35–38</strong><small>Hiranyakashipu’s requested protections and sovereignty.</small></li><li><strong>7.4.1–28</strong><small>The boon, conquest, tyranny and divine assurance concerning Prahlada.</small></li><li><strong>7.4.29–42; 7.5.1–7</strong><small>Prahlada’s qualities, inward devotion and education under Shanda and Amarka.</small></li><li><strong>7.5.12–32</strong><small>Prahlada’s answers, including the ninefold account of devotion.</small></li><li><strong>7.5.33–57</strong><small>Hiranyakashipu’s fury, the attempts on Prahlada’s life and their failure.</small></li><li><strong>7.6.1–30; 7.7.1–55</strong><small>Prahlada’s teaching to the children and his account of Narada’s instruction.</small></li><li><strong>7.8.1–16</strong><small>The final exchange, the source of strength and the question of the pillar.</small></li><li><strong>7.8.17–56</strong><small>Narasimha’s manifestation, Hiranyakashipu’s defeat and the assembled prayers.</small></li><li><strong>7.9.1–55</strong><small>Prahlada approaches, receives the Lord’s touch, prays and is offered a boon.</small></li><li><strong>7.10.1–34</strong><small>Prahlada refuses worldly reward, asks for his father, performs the rites and is installed as ruler.</small></li></ol><p>The retelling begins with the boon rather than reproducing the longer preceding genealogy and grief discourse. Philosophical teaching, prayers and combat are condensed. It does not add the popular Holika episode, which is not part of this chapter sequence in the supplied Bhagavata account.</p><p>Two reader paintings and one collection-card image are AI-generated devotional interpretations created for single placements. The four-armed Narasimha with conch and discus is a visual convention; the manifestation passage uses expansive poetic description. Violence is rendered non-graphically. “A devotion no command can own” is an editorial reflection.</p></div></section>
